WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:05.010
Don't expect a "Waititi Cut" of "Thor: Love
and Thunder" any time soon. Why were some

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:10.120
fans disappointed at notable character absences
and a suspicious lack of god-butchering? And

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:14.080
how many death fakeouts are too much? These
are the "Thor: Love and Thunder" moments that

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.550
really upset fans. Spoilers ahead.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:20.870
Christian Bale as Gorr the God Butcher in
"Thor: Love and Thunder" looks pretty cool,

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:24.840
all things considered. However, there were
some fans who were very vocally upset that

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.640
he doesn't really look like his comic book
counterpart. While Bale's portrayal of the

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:33.640
complicated villain has been largely well-received,
it makes sense why some fans would be upset.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:37.440
While comic book movies have been notorious
in the past for being lax about how they adapted

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.430
characters' backstories and looks, modern
comic book movies have, for the most part,

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:43.059
been much more faithful.

00:00:43.059 --> 00:00:46.870
Director Taika Waititi said in an interview
with IGN that he expected that fans would

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:51.670
think a more comics-accurate Gorr looked like
Voldermort. After all, his pale, bald, noseless

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:56.460
comic design does emulate the fantasy villain
quite a bit. However, at the end of the day,

00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:00.839
the Gorr in "Love and Thunder" is still undeniably
Gorr, and Waititi felt that the character's

00:01:00.839 --> 00:01:05.320
story was far more important than any visual
similarities to other fictional villains.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:10.549
"Thor: Love and Thunder" is a fun film, but
it also touches on some dark moments and themes.

00:01:10.549 --> 00:01:13.980
After all, the main villain is literally named
Gorr the God Butcher, which sounds like the

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:18.829
name of a Norwegian black metal band. As such,
fans expected to see more intense, mature

00:01:18.829 --> 00:01:22.590
scenes, some of which were apparently left
on the cutting room floor.

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:26.439
In an interview with IGN, Christian Bale talked
about one brutal sequence that never made

00:01:26.439 --> 00:01:28.180
it into the film. Bale said,

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:32.090
"[Gorr]'s a highly religious character at
the beginning with tattoos displaying his

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:35.689
piousness, and then he becomes disillusioned
with that, and then literally just mutilates

00:01:35.689 --> 00:01:39.600
himself to get rid of that ... It was perhaps
a little bit too extreme to be included in

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:42.380
the film, but there was a lot of wonderful
stuff that we shot."

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:46.120
Great scenes get deleted for a variety of
reasons, and it sounds like this one was cut

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:49.560
to make the film more broadly appealing. As
Bale said in the same interview,

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:54.929
"Some things can work, some things don't,
and we want this to be a family film. My kids

00:01:54.929 --> 00:01:56.340
love it."

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:57.760
"Let's bring the rainbow."

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:02.770
The morally ambiguous Loki is a fan favorite
in the MCU for a reason. Loki is Thor's adopted

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:08.009
evil brother and eventual arch-nemesis –- essentially
the Joker to Thor's Batman. Of course, his

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:11.700
true nature and morality became much more
fluid over the years, much like the mythical

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:14.069
Norse god with whom he shares a name.

00:02:14.069 --> 00:02:18.599
Loki made his live-action feature film debut
in 2011's "Thor" and has been in every single

00:02:18.599 --> 00:02:23.319
Thor feature up until now. He's also the star
of his own popular Disney+ show, "Loki." It's

00:02:23.319 --> 00:02:27.780
safe to say that fans like to see as much
Loki as the MCU is willing to showcase.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:31.430
Unfortunately, it makes sense that Loki is
absent from the most recent Thor film, as

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:35.129
there's really no room for him in the already
crowded narrative — not to mention he's

00:02:35.129 --> 00:02:39.620
currently considered dead to everyone in the
current universe. Still, his absence was always

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:41.569
going to upset fans no matter what.

00:02:41.569 --> 00:02:46.560
"I guess I'll just have to go it alone, like
I've always done."

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:52.769
Sif, played by actress Jaimie Alexander, was
a familiar face in the first two "Thor" films.

00:02:52.769 --> 00:02:56.360
She was originally part of Thor's group of
powerful Asgardian warriors who assisted him

00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:01.080
in fighting off frost giants and Loki in "Thor."
Those same warriors later helped bring peace

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:05.590
to the universe after the destruction of the
Bifrost in "Thor: The Dark World." Unfortunately,

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:09.980
she was mostly out of the picture after that,
to the vocal disappointment of some fans.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:13.770
As such, it was great seeing her again in
"Thor: Love and Thunder." Sadly, we don't

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:18.099
get to see her fight or really do anything
cool. Instead, we see her already defeated

00:03:18.099 --> 00:03:22.720
after having her arm cut off by Gorr the God
Butcher. Beyond that, we also find out that

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:27.361
she was only left alive by Gorr so that she
could give Thor a warning. Ultimately, it's

00:03:27.361 --> 00:03:30.659
disappointing that "Thor: Love and Thunder"
couldn't find a more interesting role for

00:03:30.659 --> 00:03:32.959
Sif to play.

00:03:32.959 --> 00:03:36.940
Fans speculated that the fan-favorite comics
character Beta Ray Bill would appear in "Thor:

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:40.799
Love and Thunder." Unfortunately, Beta Ray
Bill didn't show up at all -– not even as

00:03:40.799 --> 00:03:43.209
an Easter egg, as he did in "Thor: Ragnarok."

00:03:43.209 --> 00:03:49.489
For those who don't know, Beta Ray Bill made
his comic book debut in 1983's "Thor" #337.

00:03:49.489 --> 00:03:54.079
Beta Ray Bill was an alien warrior who -– despite
his extremely monstrous appearance –- was

00:03:54.079 --> 00:03:57.969
actually an honorable and brave hero, one
who was worthy enough to be able to wield

00:03:57.969 --> 00:03:58.969
Mjolnir.

00:03:58.969 --> 00:04:02.610
The reason why Beta Ray Bill is so popular
is not just because he was one of the first

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:07.209
characters besides Thor to wield Mjolnir in
the Marvel universe. He also contributed to

00:04:07.209 --> 00:04:11.120
the creation of the comic book version of
Stormbreaker. Hopefully, the MCU can find

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:13.620
a way to fit him in the next solo Thor adventure.

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:15.879
"Next time, baby."

00:04:15.879 --> 00:04:19.561
The ending of "Avengers: Endgame" implied
there'd be a lot of "Guardians of the Galaxy"

00:04:19.561 --> 00:04:24.200
screen time in the sequel. Some fans even
referred to the potential sequel as "Asgardians

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:28.320
of the Galaxy." Sure, that title was never
confirmed, but that just goes to show how

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:32.669
the possibility of a true "Guardians of the
Galaxy" and "Thor" team-up had some fans pretty

00:04:32.669 --> 00:04:33.669
excited.

00:04:33.669 --> 00:04:36.960
To be fair, the entire first part of the movie
is an extended montage of Thor working with

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:41.090
the famous Guardians team. Still, fans who
did want more Guardians of the Galaxy screen

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:45.180
time were undoubtedly a bit disappointed,
especially since early trailers implied they'd

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:48.650
potentially be a bigger part of the plot.

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:52.940
The death of Jane Foster, aka the Mighty Thor,
is one of those scenes that was purposely

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:57.440
meant to upset audiences. After all, Portman's
portrayal of the new Thor was charming and

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:02.139
fun, and it's sad when any fan-favorite character
sacrifices themselves to save the day.

00:05:02.139 --> 00:05:06.620
What's even more upsetting is that she begins
the movie suffering from a cancer diagnosis.

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:10.500
While Mjolnir seemingly heals her and makes
her a literal god, it is also what's stopping

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:14.710
her from healing while in human form. This
means that anytime she's out superheroing

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:19.229
with Thor as Mighty Thor, she's getting closer
and closer to death every time she lets go

00:05:19.229 --> 00:05:20.229
of the hammer.

00:05:20.229 --> 00:05:24.590
Her final sacrifice is extremely well-done,
with both Portman and Hemsworth selling the

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:29.090
sadness and beauty of her ultimate heroic
sacrifice. Still, that didn't stop some fans

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:30.600
from complaining.

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:35.910
Korg, the lovable Kronan, is at one point
seemingly killed, to the surprise of Thor

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:39.580
and his friends. This happens while the gang
heads to Omnipotence City, where the different

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:43.710
gods of the universe congregate. The heroes
are going there to try and warn Zeus about

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:46.850
Gorr the God Butcher and request the gods'
help in defeating him.

00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:50.680
However, Zeus doesn't like being ordered around
and would prefer to stay hidden in Omnipotence

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:55.980
City, which causes a fight between the Asgardians
and Zeus. During the attack, Zeus blasts Korg

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:59.220
with his famous lightning bolt, which causes
him to explode.

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:04.139
This "death" was upsetting for fans, who thought
that the film killed off such a lovable character.

00:06:04.139 --> 00:06:08.710
Luckily, Korg comes back to life, as apparently
Kronan's just need their heads to survive.

00:06:08.710 --> 00:06:13.819
Speaking of Zeus, at the end of that fight,
Thor sends one of Zeus' lightning bolts straight

00:06:13.819 --> 00:06:18.120
back into the Olympian's chest, seemingly
killing him. This allows our heroes to escape

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:21.340
from the gods' Omnipotence City with his weapon
in tow.

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:25.120
However, at the end of the film, during the
post credits scene, it's revealed that, like

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:29.210
Korg, Zeus actually survived the attack. He
talks about how the people used to worship

00:06:29.210 --> 00:06:34.069
gods but now call for superheroes in times
of need instead. We then see that he's talking

00:06:34.069 --> 00:06:39.000
to his son, Hercules, who himself is a lesser-known
superhero in the Marvel universe.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:43.319
Unfortunately, Zeus' death was so satisfying
that it's a bit upsetting that he didn't permanently

00:06:43.319 --> 00:06:47.390
pay for his arrogance. Furthermore, it's not
like there weren't other ways Hercules could've

00:06:47.390 --> 00:06:51.060
been introduced in the post-credits scene
–- it could've even happened during a funeral

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:53.522
or something, for instance.

00:06:53.522 --> 00:06:57.710
One of the best parts of Taika Waititi-directed
films is how he manages to carefully blend

00:06:57.710 --> 00:07:02.060
moments of drama with comedy. In the case
of Waititi's MCU "Thor" films, he manages

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:06.740
to blend in action and fantasy as well. When
pulled off well, this blend of seemingly disparate

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:09.760
genres can be incredibly compelling for audience
members.

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:14.180
However, that sort of tonal dexterity is hard
to pull off at the best of times, and even

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:18.770
the greatest filmmakers sometimes stumble.
Waititi is no exception. While most critics

00:07:18.770 --> 00:07:23.790
enjoyed his previous Thor film, 2017's "Thor:
Ragnarok," there were some fans who criticized

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:26.830
its overuse of comedy at the expense of genuine
emotion.

00:07:26.830 --> 00:07:30.410
Since "Thor: Love and Thunder" deals with
even heavier subjects, the margin of error

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:35.300
is even slimmer than ever before. And, according
to some fans, Waititi wasn't able to stick

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:37.860
the landing this time.

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:40.280
In an interview with IndieWire, Natalie Portman
revealed,

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:44.270
"There were whole sequences, planets, characters,
and worlds that didn't end up in the movie

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:48.180
that were hilarious and amazing and [that]
we spent a lot of time and energy on."

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:53.120
Now, deleted scenes and abandoned plots happen
with every movie, and co-writer/director Taika

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:57.870
Waititi responded to a potential "Waititi
Cut" with some harsh words. He told NME,

00:07:57.870 --> 00:08:02.090
"I've been thinking about director's cuts.
I watch director's cuts of a lot of other

00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:05.850
directors. They suck. Director's cuts are
not good. Directors need to be controlled

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:10.389
sometimes and if I was to say, 'Ah, you wanna
watch my director's cut? It's four and a half

00:08:10.389 --> 00:08:14.340
hours long!' It's not good ... There's a lot
of cup-of-tea breaks in there."

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:18.849
But is that true? Were all the best ideas
shown on screen? As we've mentioned before,

00:08:18.849 --> 00:08:24.460
there is a curious lack of actual god butchering
by the supposed "Gorr the God Butcher." Furthermore,

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:28.160
extending the runtime would allow us to see
more of the Marvel Cosmos, as well as tie

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:32.590
up loose ends with characters from previous
films. Those would include, according to Christian

00:08:32.590 --> 00:08:36.790
Bale, Jeff Goldblum's Grandmaster and Peter
Dinklage's Dwarf Eitri.

00:08:36.790 --> 00:08:40.960
Heck, Waititi wouldn't even need to necessarily
add to the runtime. He could have culled other

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:44.890
plot lines, possibly shortening the kidnapping
thread or trimming the time spent with Thor

00:08:44.890 --> 00:08:49.670
hanging out with the Guardians of the Galaxy.
This could have allowed more time to let Gorr's

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:51.000
arc breathe.

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:55.530
After four phases of grand, intertwined storytelling,
the MCU rightfully deserves praise for its

00:08:55.530 --> 00:09:00.450
unprecedented success, especially compared
to other would-be franchises. Anyone remember

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:01.450
"The Dark Universe?"

00:09:01.450 --> 00:09:06.920
"I'm offering you a partnership. You, evil
incarnate, me, your good friend Eddie Hyde."

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:10.960
However, there is nonetheless a large portion
of the fanbase who feel that the most recent

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:15.670
slate of Marvel movies have been kind of rudderless.
And this makes some sense, as the previous

00:09:15.670 --> 00:09:20.470
phases spent many years leading towards the
ultimate conflict with Thanos and the universe-altering

00:09:20.470 --> 00:09:24.720
Infinity Stones. Having such a clear endgame
in mind meant that even weaker installments

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:26.790
of the MCU had a sense of purpose.

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:30.970
To be fair, Phase Four of the MCU has seen
an influx of multiverse stories, which seems

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:35.890
to be the new era's endgame. This started
with "Loki" on Disney+, later becoming a main

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:39.670
focus for the plots of "Spider-Man: No Way
Home," "Doctor Strange in the Multiverse of

00:09:39.670 --> 00:09:44.540
Madness," and presumably the upcoming "Ant-Man:
Quantumania." However, "Thor: Love and Thunder"

00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:46.440
didn't touch on any of that at all.

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:50.230
Now, it's not like the film had to address
the multiverse, necessarily. There are certainly

00:09:50.230 --> 00:09:54.510
some fans who prefer standalone superhero
adventures existing alongside the inevitable

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:58.910
team-ups and crossovers. Still, it's a definite
change from the tight plotting that defined

00:09:58.910 --> 00:10:04.270
the earlier eras of the MCU, making "Love
and Thunder" feel somehow off.

